EXT. RIVER BOATHOUSE - DAWN

Mist rising off a flat brown river. Racing shells rest on racks like sleeping insects. Dew on the dock boards. A single crow on a piling.

COACH ARCHIBALD PELHAM-GREENE, 60s, tweed jacket and megaphone, stands at the end of the dock. JASPER OKUMURA, 20s, stroke, sits on the edge with his oar across his knees. DOLORES RIVAS, 20s, coxswain, tiny in an oversized windbreaker, studies a clipboard. ANSELM TREMAINE, 20s, bow, ties his shoes with great ceremony.

ARCHIBALD
Eights in five.

DOLORES
We're ready.

ARCHIBALD
Are you?

JASPER
Yes, Coach.

ARCHIBALD
Anselm?

ANSELM
Yes, Coach.

ARCHIBALD
You look like a man tying his shoes.

ANSELM
I am.

ARCHIBALD
Tie them faster.

Anselm ties them slower. Dolores stifles a laugh.

ARCHIBALD (CONT'D)
Regatta in nine days. Their stroke rate is thirty-eight.

JASPER
Ours is thirty-six.

ARCHIBALD
Ours is thirty-six and clean.

JASPER
Clean wins.

ARCHIBALD
Clean wins eventually.

Dolores raises her hand.

DOLORES
Coach, can I say something?

ARCHIBALD
You may.

DOLORES
I've been counting strokes since March.

ARCHIBALD
And?

DOLORES
We're never as bad as we think we are.

A silence. Archibald looks at the river. The crow flies off.

ARCHIBALD
That's the best thing anyone's said all season.

He raps the megaphone on the post.

ARCHIBALD (CONT'D)
Boat on the water.

They stand. Four people lift a shell with the unhurried care of men carrying a coffin. Nobody speaks. The mist parts.
